I considered at one point titling this issue `Nested computed signals should be forbidden` but then I realized I couldn't survive if they were - even for some of the simplest of cases! I do fully understand all that you're saying though and there are clearly many DX and performance implications of any final decision. Wherever possible it's better to inform developers with warnings or errors about bad code they may have written, but as soon as you put up a solid wall it's going to break somebody's code.

### My most complicated use case

The code where I first came across this issue is quite complex and as I mentioned it was converted from RxJS (lots of `combineLatest` and `switchMap`). So I'm providing this overview mainly for curiosity - and then I'll move on to some more simplified and general thoughts.

I have components with dynamic config (from a JSON file) and that config can be updated (using Microsoft SignalR) while the page is running (via my own page editor). So if I update the design on my laptop my phone will instantly update by updating all the config signals. One way I've benefit from memoized nested `computed` signals is when accessing a single config property (maybe a boolean) which gets memoized in-place automatically without having to declare it 'outside'. This can help minimize recomputation or even refreshing of the whole component if other unconnected parts of the config change. It's just easier and cleaner to access the config in-place where I consume it, rather than 'outside' the computed. This notably never happens for the end user, their config never changes. So that's one way this behavior can benefit me, but not really useful as a general argument against nested computed signals because I could easily cache it 'outside' if I had to.

### Thoughts about APIs that use or return Signals

Consider a trivial API computation in a general third party / application specific service that consumes at least one global signal and returns a value. You first need to decide whether the API will return `Signal<T>` or just `T`. 

Return `Signal<T>`  (which is a `computed`)
- It's obvious that the value returned is reactive.
- The value returned can be cached easily (as a Signal).
- The value is already memoized.
- The value can be easily used in a page template.
- However the API call (that returns `computed`) CANNOT safely be made from inside another computed. (the crux of this issue)

Return just `T`
- The value is ready to use without dereferencing it.
- It's not necessarily obvious if the API call has consumed any signals (because it's just a boolean or an object). 
- The API call CAN safely be made inside a new computed. (because it can track all the dependencies)

You may even choose to offer both these options!
And projects like ngrx may have to end up doing so if nested computed were forbidden. 

I think on the whole API designers will choose to return a `Signal<T>` because it's more explicit and seems correct.

### Benefits of simply forbidding inner `computed`
- Users will quickly be able to see if they are recreating a signal on every iteration of their computation. That signal often can be moved outside to improve performance.
- Reduce graph churn, memory pressure and GC issues. This is especially an issue for a computation that gets run frequently or creates many inner `computed` signals.

### Disadvantages of forbidding inner computed
- Some code may end up harder to read if dependencies need to be declared 'outside'.
- Developers may end up having to provide `Signal<T>` and `T` return types from utility methods (as described above), adding complexity and confusion.
- It may be impossible to cleanly refactor code that creates a `computed` signal if the computation has both internal signal dependencies and requires inputs derived from application code signals. (I'll try to explain this below!)

I've no doubt that forbidding inner computed would suddenly break a lot of code (very curious about this one). I'm probably more convinced that it would fix a lot of silly mistakes. But I don't think it's feasible. 

### Use case for creating a Signal inside a computed
One example where I would have difficulty moving my dependencies 'outside' is for a list of dynamic components where the visibility of each is determined by a service. 

Consider an oversimplified 'visibility service' that determine if a page element (in a list of page elements) can be displayed or not. It might be based on screen width, global configuration, user role or AB testing settings.

The simplest implementation might look like this. 

```
class VisibilityService 
{
    // may depend on
    screenWidth: Signal<number>;
    isVideoEnabled: Signal<boolean>;
    userRole: Signal<string>;

    isVisible: (section: string) => boolean 
    isVisibleSignal: (section: string) => Signal<boolean> 
}
```

I've included methods with `boolean` and `Signal<boolean>` return types. They both internally run the same computation. 

eg. `isVisibleSignal(section: string) { return computed(() => this.isVisible(section)) }`

We then create a simple page model, passed to the template and displayed in an `*ngFor`.

```
// elements on the page
const page = signal( ['header', 'hero', 'video', 'footer'] );

const pageModel = computed(() => {

    return page().map(section =>
    {
        return {
            componentName: section,
            visible: visibilityService.isVisible(section)    
        }
    });
});
```

This is the simplest version, using `isVisible(string) => boolean`.  So that means no new `computed` is created by `visibilityService`, and any dependencies used by it are tracked by our `computed`. 

We could stop right here and probably never notice a problem :-) 

Now, if the calculation happens to depend on screen width you'd see terrible performance. So we need to switch to using the memoized value returned from `isVisibleSignal()` instead.

```
return page().map(section =>
{
    return {
        componentName: section,
        visible: visibilityService.isVisibleSignal(section)    
    }
});
```

This version creates new `computed` signals for each section, but never actually accesses them. Since it returns the signals they don't get garbage collected. 

Of course, now we need to use `visible()` inside the template (which is fine). Another benefit is that the `pageModel` computation (that creates all the visible signals) only gets executed if `page()` changes and not every time the visibility of an individual section changes.

If Angular had imposed a hard and fast rule `No computed can be created inside a computation` then this version would not be allowed (unfairly so I think). 

To trigger the GC issue we'd need to dereference these inner computed values:

```
return page().map(section =>
{
    return {
        componentName: section,
        visible: visibilityService.isVisibleSignal(section) ()
    }
});
```

Now we're back where we started (!) with an inner computed that suddenly stops working when GC kicks in. This version helps demonstrate its own inherent inefficiency since every visibility signal is recreated each time the visibility of any section changes. With a couple dozen sections could be very bad.

While we want to avoid creating new `computed` inside a computation as much as possible, we ultimately have a sort of chicken-and-egg problem:

* To get the list of page sections (which may change) we have to access `page()`
* To be reactive, the call to `page()` needs to be inside a `computed`
* To call the visibility service we need the value `section`
* But `section` only exists inside the `computed`
* So we must call the visibility service inside `computed`
* Now we have GC issues and graph side effects

Another approach might be to try and do it in two stages:

```
const page = signal( ['header', 'hero', 'video', 'footer'] );

// Create the page model with signals for visibility
const pageModel_intermediate = computed(() => {

    return page().map(section =>
    {
        return {
            componentName: section,

            // create the signal, but don't call it
            visible: visibilityService.isVisibleSignal(section)
        }
    });
});

// Step 2: Final model
const pageModel = computed(() => {

    const sections = pageModel_intermediate().map(section =>
    {
        return {
            ...section,
            visible: section.visible()
        }
    });

    // filter out only visible sections
    const visibleSections = sections.filter(s => s.visible);
    return visibleSections;
});
```

This actually works quite nicely: 

- Signals created by `visibilityService` are only created inside `pageModel_intermediate` when `page(s)` changes
- The final `pageModel` properly tracks all the `visible` signals as dependencies
- No GC issue
- No major graph issue. If `pages()` changes then all the previous signals are disconnected.

The problem here of course is:

- If Angular were to forbid `computed()` being called from a computation then we couldn't even do this
- Developers need to understand this whole issue to have got this far in the first place!

### Conclusions

- I don't think it's going to be possible for Angular to forbid the creation of nested/inner `computed` without a lot of pushback. 
- GC issue could be fixed if a `computed` computation were to track its own `ComputedImpl` dependencies and cache them with a strong reference.
- If Angular were to attempt to forbid inner `computed` solely to avoid the GC issue (unlikely) it would theoretically need to only forbid accessing newly created `computed` signals. But that sounds completely impractical and not worthy of further consideration.
- Whatever the outcome it may likely require an opt in option (similar to `allowSignalWrites`). This would help provide guidance and understanding of what's going on and prevent many unintentional nested `computed` signals.

@markostanimirovic Generally no, there not an absolute need in what I've encountered so far to use nested signals. So I don't really set out to intentionally to 'create nested signals'. It's always as a side effect of trying to use a cached / memoized calculation. And as you mentioned, caching `selectSignal` doesn't ultimately solve this (except the GC issue). 

I like the sound of `(pure) memoized function` but that's sort of what I thought I was doing! 

Can you give an example of what you mean by that?

